1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,840
This is the Football Intelligence Platform.

2
00:00:04,190 --> 00:00:11,150
An AI-first football analytics project that goes from raw match data to an explainable prediction on your phone.

3
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:20,540
Results for Europe's top five leagues come from football-data.co.uk, and season schedules from openfootball.

4
00:00:20,890 --> 00:00:24,990
Both are validated and versioned before anything uses them.

5
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:33,090
An XGBoost model, trained on twenty-two seasons of matches, predicts a home win, a draw or an away win.

6
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:39,970
SHAP explains every prediction, and a Dixon-Coles goals model adds the likely scores.

7
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:46,850
A FastAPI backend serves all of it, with interactive documentation.

8
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:56,300
Version two is the current API. Version one still answers exactly as the first release did, so older clients keep working.

9
00:00:57,710 --> 00:01:04,310
Ask for Arsenal against Chelsea, and the server builds all forty-two model features from match history.

10
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:12,220
The model gives Arsenal a sixty-five percent chance of winning. The draw is not tagged as likely here.

11
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:18,250
The explain endpoint says why, in plain football language.

12
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:24,960
Arsenal's team strength rating and their sixty-eight percent win rate at home push hardest toward the pick.

13
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:33,740
Ask version one the same question, and the original Premier League model from June answers, in the original format.

14
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:41,640
Upcoming fixtures for every league are refreshed daily, and each kick-off time carries its time zone.

15
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:46,160
The assistant runs locally with Ollama.

16
00:01:46,510 --> 00:01:51,430
It answers only from the project's own documents, and names its sources.

17
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:58,020
The Android app opens on upcoming fixtures, with one tab per league.

18
00:01:58,370 --> 00:02:02,210
Kick-off times are shown in the phone's own time zone.

19
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:07,260
The bottom bar leads to predictions, the assistant and settings.

20
00:02:08,670 --> 00:02:12,170
To predict, pick a league and two teams.

21
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:19,550
The result shows the win, draw and loss probabilities, the most likely scores, and the goal markets.

22
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:25,900
Explain lists the factors that pushed the model toward its pick, and the ones that pushed against it.

23
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:34,380
Every answer is saved on the phone. Here the server has been stopped, and we pull to refresh.

24
00:02:34,730 --> 00:02:41,910
Once the request times out, the app keeps showing the saved fixtures, under a banner that says when they were saved.

25
00:02:43,310 --> 00:02:48,470
Settings shows which backend services are up, including the assistant.

26
00:02:49,880 --> 00:02:54,270
Everything runs on a laptop, with no cloud services.

27
00:02:54,620 --> 00:02:59,640
The code, documentation and every design decision are on GitHub.
